/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Jeremiah chapters 1-5
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("jeremiah", [
{n:1, name:"The Call of Jeremiah", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 1:5",verse:"Before I formed thee in the belly I knew thee; and before thou camest forth out of the womb I sanctified thee, and I ordained thee a prophet unto the nations."}, facts:["Did you know the almond tree was God's wordplay? In Hebrew, 'almond' (shaqed) sounds like 'I will hasten, or watch' (shoqed), so the rod of an almond tree meant God was watching over His word to perform it (Jeremiah 1:11-12).","Did you know Jeremiah's ministry spanned about forty years, from the thirteenth year of Josiah to the carrying away of Jerusalem captive in the fifth month (Jeremiah 1:2-3)?","Did you know the LORD touched Jeremiah's mouth and put His words in it, the same pattern as Isaiah's call, so the prophet's message was God's own (Jeremiah 1:9)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who was Jeremiah's father?", options:["Hilkiah, of the priests in Anathoth","Shallum, of the scribes in Jerusalem","Ahikam, of the princes of Judah","Gemariah, of the keepers of the gate"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:1",verse:"The words of Jeremiah the son of Hilkiah, of the priests that were in Anathoth in the land of Benjamin:", insight:"God raises prophets from faithful families; Hilkiah's priestly home in Anathoth was the cradle of a weeping prophet."},
{q:"In which land was Jeremiah's hometown of Anathoth?", options:["The land of Benjamin","The land of Ephraim","The land of Naphtali","The land of Zebulun"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:1",verse:"The words of Jeremiah the son of Hilkiah, of the priests that were in Anathoth in the land of Benjamin:", insight:"Anathoth lay in Benjamin's hills, a priestly town near Jerusalem; God often calls His messengers from quiet places."},
{q:"In whose reign did the word of the LORD first come to Jeremiah?", options:["Josiah, in the thirteenth year of his reign","Jehoiakim, in the first year of his reign","Zedekiah, in the eleventh year of his reign","Manasseh, in the years of his old age"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:2",verse:"To whom the word of the LORD came in the days of Josiah the son of Amon king of Judah, in the thirteenth year of his reign.", insight:"Josiah's revival was underway when Jeremiah began; God's word comes right on time, in the thirteenth year of a good king."},
{q:"How far did Jeremiah's ministry of the word run?", options:["Through the days of Jehoiakim and Zedekiah, unto Jerusalem's captivity in the fifth month","Only through the reign of Josiah","From the exile to the return","Forty days in the wilderness of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:3",verse:"It came also in the days of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah, unto the end of the eleventh year of Zedekiah the son of Josiah king of Judah, unto the carrying away of Jerusalem captive in the fifth month.", insight:"From Josiah's revival to Jerusalem's ruin, Jeremiah carried the word for decades; faithfulness is measured in years, not moments."},
{q:"How does Jeremiah's call begin?", options:["The word of the LORD came unto him, saying","A fire fell from heaven upon him","He dreamed a dream at Bethel","A seraph touched his lips with a coal"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:4",verse:"Then the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,", insight:"Prophecy begins not with a man's ambition but with God's word arriving; the calling finds the called."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah's first excuse?", options:["I cannot speak: for I am a child","I am too old to carry this burden","I do not know the law of Moses","I have no sign to show the people"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:6",verse:"Then said I, Ah, Lord GOD! behold, I cannot speak: for I am a child.", insight:"'I am a child' is the oldest excuse in the book; God does not ask for your ability, He supplies it."},
{q:"What did the LORD forbid Jeremiah to say?", options:["Say not, I am a child","Say not, the people are too far gone","Say not, the task is too great","Say not, the kings will not hear"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:7",verse:"But the LORD said unto me, Say not, I am a child: for thou shalt go to all that I shall send thee, and whatsoever I command thee thou shalt speak.", insight:"God vetoes our self dismissal; 'Say not, I am a child' is heaven's refusal to accept your excuse."},
{q:"Why was Jeremiah not to fear their faces?", options:["For I am with thee to deliver thee, saith the LORD","For the kings would protect him","For he would preach only in secret","For his youth would win their pity"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:8",verse:"Be not afraid of their faces: for I am with thee to deliver thee, saith the LORD.", insight:"Faces are frightening until you remember who stands behind you; 'I am with thee' turns fear into courage."},
{q:"What did the LORD do to Jeremiah's mouth?", options:["He put forth His hand and touched it, putting His words in it","He sealed it with silence for seven days","He washed it with water from the temple","He marked it with hyssop and oil"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:9",verse:"Then the LORD put forth his hand, and touched my mouth. And the LORD said unto me, Behold, I have put my words in thy mouth.", insight:"God's hand on the mouth is God's word in the mouth; the prophet speaks heaven's lines, not his own."},
{q:"Over what was Jeremiah set?", options:["Over the nations and over the kingdoms","Over the temple and its vessels","Over the armies of Judah","Over the schools of the prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:10",verse:"See, I have this day set thee over the nations and over the kingdoms, to root out, and to pull down, and to destroy, and to throw down, to build, and to plant.", insight:"A young man from Anathoth was set over nations and kingdoms; God's assignments are always bigger than the assignee."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah appointed to do first?", options:["To root out, and to pull down, and to destroy, and to throw down","To bless, and to prosper, and to multiply","To gather, and to heal, and to comfort","To watch, and to wait, and to be silent"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:10",verse:"See, I have this day set thee over the nations and over the kingdoms, to root out, and to pull down, and to destroy, and to throw down, to build, and to plant.", insight:"Before God builds, He demolishes; tearing down false hopes is the first mercy of the word."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah see in his first vision?", options:["A rod of an almond tree","A seething pot toward the north","A basket of summer fruit","A lampstand of pure gold"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:11",verse:"Moreover the word of the LORD came unto me, saying, Jeremiah, what seest thou? And I said, I see a rod of an almond tree.", insight:"An almond branch, the first tree to wake in spring; God was showing that His word was about to blossom into action."},
{q:"What did God promise about His word?", options:["I will hasten my word to perform it","I will hide my word from the nations","I will delay my word for a season","I will speak only in parables"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:12",verse:"Then said the LORD unto me, Thou hast well seen: for I will hasten my word to perform it.", insight:"God watches over His word the way spring watches an almond tree; what He says, He hastens to do."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah see the second time?", options:["A seething pot, with its face toward the north","A whirlwind coming from the south","A great eagle over the temple","A plumbline in the LORD's hand"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:13",verse:"And the word of the LORD came unto me the second time, saying, What seest thou? And I said, I see a seething pot; and the face thereof is toward the north.", insight:"A boiling pot tipping from the north; judgment was already on the fire, and Judah was in the pot."},
{q:"From where would evil break forth?", options:["Out of the north, upon all the inhabitants of the land","Out of Egypt, upon the cities of Judah","Out of the sea, upon the coastlands","Out of the wilderness, upon the shepherds"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:14",verse:"Then the LORD said unto me, Out of the north an evil shall break forth upon all the inhabitants of the land.", insight:"The north held Babylon; God names the direction of danger plainly so His people cannot plead surprise."},
{q:"Where would the northern kings set their thrones?", options:["At the entering of the gates of Jerusalem","In the courts of the temple","On the plains of Megiddo","Beside the river Euphrates"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:15",verse:"For, lo, I will call all the families of the kingdoms of the north, saith the LORD; and they shall come, and they shall set every one his throne at the entering of the gates of Jerusalem, and against all the walls thereof round about, and against all the cities of Judah.", insight:"Enemy thrones at Jerusalem's gates; when God is forsaken, the city's entrances become a conqueror's court."},
{q:"Why would God utter His judgments against them?", options:["They had forsaken Him and burned incense to other gods","They had forgotten the feast of unleavened bread","They had built houses of cedar","They had neglected the temple tax"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:16",verse:"And I will utter my judgments against them touching all their wickedness, who have forsaken me, and have burned incense unto other gods, and worshipped the works of their own hands.", insight:"Judgment has a reason: forsaking God and worshipping the works of their own hands; sin is the lawsuit's whole evidence."},
{q:"What did God command Jeremiah to do?", options:["Gird up thy loins, and arise, and speak all that I command thee","Flee to the mountains and hide","Gather silver for the temple","Write the law upon tablets of stone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:17",verse:"Thou therefore gird up thy loins, and arise, and speak unto them all that I command thee: be not dismayed at their faces, lest I confound thee before them.", insight:"Girded loins mean a worker ready; the prophet's courage is simply obedience in work clothes."},
{q:"What did God make Jeremiah that day?", options:["A defenced city, an iron pillar, and brasen walls","A swift messenger with wings as eagles","A hidden treasure in a secret place","A burning lamp in a dark house"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:18",verse:"For, behold, I have made thee this day a defenced city, and an iron pillar, and brasen walls against the whole land, against the kings of Judah, against the princes thereof, against the priests thereof, and against the people of the land.", insight:"God fortifies the faithful; a defenced city, an iron pillar, brasen walls, against kings, priests, and people."},
{q:"What promise closed Jeremiah's call?", options:["They shall fight against thee, but they shall not prevail against thee","They shall love thee and follow thy word","They shall crown thee king in Jerusalem","They shall build thee a house of cedar"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 1:19",verse:"And they shall fight against thee; but they shall not prevail against thee; for I am with thee, saith the LORD, to deliver thee.", insight:"They will fight, but they will not prevail; opposition is promised, and so is deliverance."}
]},
{n:2, name:"The Two Evils", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 2:13",verse:"For my people have committed two evils; they have forsaken me the fountain of living waters, and hewed them out cisterns, broken cisterns, that can hold no water."}, facts:["Did you know God names His people's twin sin as 'two evils': forsaking Him, the fountain of living waters, and hewing out broken cisterns that can hold no water (Jeremiah 2:13)?","Did you know Noph and Tahapanes were Egyptian cities? The ally Judah trusted for help is the one that 'broke the crown of thy head' (Jeremiah 2:16).","Did you know God pictures backsliding Israel as a swift dromedary and a wild ass snuffing the wind, images of restless, ungovernable desire (Jeremiah 2:23-24)?"], questions:[
{q:"In whose ears must Jeremiah cry the word?", options:["In the ears of Jerusalem","In the ears of the king of Egypt","In the ears of the priests of Bethel","In the ears of the elders of Samaria"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:2",verse:"Go and cry in the ears of Jerusalem, saying, Thus saith the LORD; I remember thee, the kindness of thy youth, the love of thine espousals, when thou wentest after me in the wilderness, in a land that was not sown.", insight:"The word is cried in Jerusalem's ears; judgment starts at home, with the city that should have known better."},
{q:"What does the LORD remember of Israel's youth?", options:["The kindness of thy youth, the love of thine espousals","The strength of thy armies in battle","The riches of thy merchants","The wisdom of thy elders"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:2",verse:"Go and cry in the ears of Jerusalem, saying, Thus saith the LORD; I remember thee, the kindness of thy youth, the love of thine espousals, when thou wentest after me in the wilderness, in a land that was not sown.", insight:"God remembers the honeymoon, when Israel followed Him into the wilderness; He pleads from love, not only from law."},
{q:"What was Israel to the LORD?", options:["Holiness unto the LORD, the firstfruits of His increase","A hired servant among the nations","A forgotten tribe of the wilderness","A treasure hidden in Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:3",verse:"Israel was holiness unto the LORD, and the firstfruits of his increase: all that devour him shall offend; evil shall come upon them, saith the LORD.", insight:"Israel was set apart like firstfruits, holy to the LORD; privilege is a calling, not a possession."},
{q:"What searching question does God ask His people?", options:["What iniquity have your fathers found in me?","Why have you built me no temple?","Where is the ark of my covenant?","Who among you keeps my sabbaths?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:5",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, What iniquity have your fathers found in me, that they are gone far from me, and have walked after vanity, and are become vain?", insight:"God asks for the indictment against Him, and there is none; 'what iniquity' exposes that the fault was never His."},
{q:"What question did the people never ask?", options:["Where is the LORD that brought us up out of the land of Egypt?","Where is the manna that we ate?","Where are the spies that we sent?","Where is the ark that we lost?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:6",verse:"Neither said they, Where is the LORD that brought us up out of the land of Egypt, that led us through the wilderness, through a land of deserts and of pits, through a land of drought, and of the shadow of death, through a land that no man passed through, and where no man dwelt?", insight:"They forgot to ask for the God who brought them out; gratitude has a short memory when the wilderness ends."},
{q:"What did Israel do with the plentiful country?", options:["They defiled God's land and made His heritage an abomination","They divided it fairly among the tribes","They planted vineyards for the poor","They built altars on every hill to the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:7",verse:"And I brought you into a plentiful country, to eat the fruit thereof and the goodness thereof; but when ye entered, ye defiled my land, and made mine heritage an abomination.", insight:"God gave a plentiful country and got back a polluted one; blessings misused become the evidence against us."},
{q:"By whom did the prophets prophesy?", options:["By Baal","By the LORD of hosts","By dreams alone","By the Urim and Thummim"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:8",verse:"The priests said not, Where is the LORD? and they that handle the law knew me not: the pastors also transgressed against me, and the prophets prophesied by Baal, and walked after things that do not profit.", insight:"Prophets prophesying by Baal is ministry with the wrong master; the name on the sermon matters."},
{q:"How far will the LORD's pleading reach?", options:["To them and to their children's children","Only to the priests of that generation","To the kings of Judah alone","To the remnant in Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:9",verse:"Wherefore I will yet plead with you, saith the LORD, and with your children’s children will I plead.", insight:"God pleads with children's children; His patience outlives generations, though His judgment will not be mocked forever."},
{q:"Which places must they survey to find such a thing?", options:["The isles of Chittim and Kedar","The mountains of Moab and Ammon","The cities of Tyre and Sidon","The rivers of Damascus"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:10",verse:"For pass over the isles of Chittim, and see; and send unto Kedar, and consider diligently, and see if there be such a thing.", insight:"Check east and west, Chittim and Kedar; no nation ever traded its gods the way Israel traded her glory."},
{q:"What had God's people changed?", options:["Their glory for that which doth not profit","Their silver for gold","Their swords for plowshares","Their tents for houses of cedar"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:11",verse:"Hath a nation changed their gods, which are yet no gods? but my people have changed their glory for that which doth not profit.", insight:"They swapped glory for garbage; idols promise everything and profit nothing."},
{q:"What did the young lions do to Israel?", options:["Roared upon him, made his land waste, and burned his cities","Guarded his borders from invaders","Carried his children to safety","Slept at the gates of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:15",verse:"The young lions roared upon him, and yelled, and they made his land waste: his cities are burned without inhabitant.", insight:"The lions roared and the cities burned; forsaking God leaves the flock open to every predator."},
{q:"Whose children broke the crown of thy head?", options:["The children of Noph and Tahapanes","The children of Chittim and Kedar","The children of Assyria and Babylon","The children of Moab and Ammon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:16",verse:"Also the children of Noph and Tahapanes have broken the crown of thy head.", insight:"Noph and Tahapanes were Egyptian cities; the ally they trusted broke their crown. False friends wound deepest."},
{q:"Who procured Israel's trouble?", options:["They procured it themselves by forsaking the LORD","The king of Babylon procured it","The prophets of Baal procured it","The sins of their fathers alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:17",verse:"Hast thou not procured this unto thyself, in that thou hast forsaken the LORD thy God, when he led thee by the way?", insight:"'Hast thou not procured this unto thyself?' Their trouble was homemade; sin is its own architect."},
{q:"Which waters are named in their alliances?", options:["The waters of Sihor and the waters of the river","The waters of Jordan and Kishon","The waters of Jabbok and Arnon","The waters of Siloam and Bethesda"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:18",verse:"And now what hast thou to do in the way of Egypt, to drink the waters of Sihor? or what hast thou to do in the way of Assyria, to drink the waters of the river?", insight:"Sihor is Egypt's Nile, the river is Assyria's Euphrates; they drank from every stream but the fountain."},
{q:"What shall correct and reprove them?", options:["Their own wickedness and their backslidings","The armies of the north","The counsel of the elders","The famine in the land"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:19",verse:"Thine own wickedness shall correct thee, and thy backslidings shall reprove thee: know therefore and see that it is an evil thing and bitter, that thou hast forsaken the LORD thy God, and that my fear is not in thee, saith the Lord GOD of hosts.", insight:"'Thine own wickedness shall correct thee'; sin carries its own rod, and backsliding writes its own rebuke."},
{q:"What had God planted Israel to be?", options:["A noble vine, wholly a right seed","A cedar of Lebanon","A fruitful olive tree","A garden of spices"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:21",verse:"Yet I had planted thee a noble vine, wholly a right seed: how then art thou turned into the degenerate plant of a strange vine unto me?", insight:"God planted a noble vine from the finest stock; nothing in Israel's ruin can be blamed on the Gardener."},
{q:"What could not wash away their iniquity?", options:["Nitre and much soap","The water of the laver","The blood of bulls","The ashes of the heifer"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:22",verse:"For though thou wash thee with nitre, and take thee much sope, yet thine iniquity is marked before me, saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"Nitre and soap were the strongest cleansers known, yet guilt stayed marked; only God can wash what soap cannot reach."},
{q:"To what is the house of Israel likened?", options:["A thief ashamed when he is found","A lion caught in a pit","A servant beaten by his master","A merchant with false balances"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:26",verse:"As the thief is ashamed when he is found, so is the house of Israel ashamed; they, their kings, their princes, and their priests, and their prophets,", insight:"Like a thief caught red handed, Israel stood ashamed; sin always looks smaller until it is found out."},
{q:"What did they say to a stock and a stone?", options:["To a stock, Thou art my father; to a stone, Thou hast brought me forth","To a stock, Thou art my servant; to a stone, Thou art my shield","To a stock, Thou art my king; to a stone, Thou art my judge","To a stock, Thou art my brother; to a stone, Thou art my friend"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:27",verse:"Saying to a stock, Thou art my father; and to a stone, Thou hast brought me forth: for they have turned their back unto me, and not their face: but in the time of their trouble they will say, Arise, and save us.", insight:"They called wood 'father' and stone 'mother'; idolatry is family language given to dead things."},
{q:"What did the people declare?", options:["We are lords; we will come no more unto thee","We are servants; command us and we obey","We are lost; have mercy upon us","We are weak; strengthen our hands"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 2:31",verse:"O generation, see ye the word of the LORD. Have I been a wilderness unto Israel? a land of darkness? wherefore say my people, We are lords; we will come no more unto thee?", insight:"'We are lords' is the proudest sentence in the chapter; independence from God is the first step into ruin."}
]},
{n:3, name:"Return, Backsliding Israel", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 3:14",verse:"Turn, O backsliding children, saith the LORD; for I am married unto you: and I will take you one of a city, and two of a family, and I will bring you to Zion:"}, facts:["Did you know God gave backsliding Israel a bill of divorce, yet still called her back, showing a mercy that outruns even lawful judgment (Jeremiah 3:8, 12)?","Did you know God says 'I am married unto you,' which is why idolatry in Jeremiah is called adultery; the covenant is a marriage, and false gods are unfaithfulness (Jeremiah 3:14, 20)?","Did you know a day is coming when no one will even mention the ark of the covenant, because Jerusalem itself shall be called the throne of the LORD (Jeremiah 3:16-17)?"], questions:[
{q:"What question opens the chapter about divorce?", options:["If a man put away his wife and she become another man's, shall he return unto her again?","If a man find fault in his wife, shall he sell her?","If a wife leave her husband, shall the elders stone her?","If a man take two wives, shall he love them equally?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:1",verse:"They say, If a man put away his wife, and she go from him, and become another man’s, shall he return unto her again? shall not that land be greatly polluted? but thou hast played the harlot with many lovers; yet return again to me, saith the LORD.", insight:"The law said a divorced wife wed to another could not return; God raises the hardest case to show the wonder of His mercy."},
{q:"What invitation follows Israel's harlotry with many lovers?", options:["Yet return again to me, saith the LORD","Yet I will hide my face from thee","Yet thou shalt be cast out forever","Yet the land shall be desolate"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:1",verse:"They say, If a man put away his wife, and she go from him, and become another man’s, shall he return unto her again? shall not that land be greatly polluted? but thou hast played the harlot with many lovers; yet return again to me, saith the LORD.", insight:"After the worst record comes the tenderest word: 'yet return again to me.' Grace outruns the law's logic."},
{q:"What is Israel told to lift her eyes and see?", options:["The high places, to see where she had not been lien with","The stars of heaven, to count their number","The temple courts, to remember her vows","The eastern mountains, to watch for dawn"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:2",verse:"Lift up thine eyes unto the high places, and see where thou hast not been lien with. In the ways hast thou sat for them, as the Arabian in the wilderness; and thou hast polluted the land with thy whoredoms and with thy wickedness.", insight:"Look at the high places; the evidence of unfaithfulness was everywhere, and God asks her to face it."},
{q:"To whom is Israel compared, waiting in the ways?", options:["The Arabian in the wilderness","The merchant in the marketplace","The watchman on the wall","The shepherd in the field"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:2",verse:"Lift up thine eyes unto the high places, and see where thou hast not been lien with. In the ways hast thou sat for them, as the Arabian in the wilderness; and thou hast polluted the land with thy whoredoms and with thy wickedness.", insight:"Like a desert bandit lying in wait, Israel sat in the ways for lovers; the picture is meant to sting."},
{q:"What had Israel refused to do?", options:["To be ashamed","To offer sacrifice","To keep the feasts","To pay the temple tax"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:3",verse:"Therefore the showers have been withholden, and there hath been no latter rain; and thou hadst a whore’s forehead, thou refusedst to be ashamed.", insight:"She had a whore's forehead and refused to be ashamed; shamelessness is the last stage of a hardened heart."},
{q:"What would Israel cry to God?", options:["My father, thou art the guide of my youth","My king, thou art the strength of my arm","My shepherd, thou art the keeper of my flock","My judge, thou art the avenger of my cause"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:4",verse:"Wilt thou not from this time cry unto me, My father, thou art the guide of my youth?", insight:"Even in rebellion she remembered the language of childhood faith; 'guide of my youth' is a memory of better days."},
{q:"In whose days did this word about backsliding Israel come?", options:["In the days of Josiah the king","In the days of Jehoiakim the king","In the days of Zedekiah the king","In the days of Hezekiah the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:6",verse:"The LORD said also unto me in the days of Josiah the king, Hast thou seen that which backsliding Israel hath done? she is gone up upon every high mountain and under every green tree, and there hath played the harlot.", insight:"Even in Josiah's revival, God was naming the rot; reform on the surface had not reached the heart."},
{q:"How did Israel answer God's call to turn?", options:["She returned not","She wept and repented","She sent gifts to the temple","She fled to the mountains"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:7",verse:"And I said after she had done all these things, Turn thou unto me. But she returned not. And her treacherous sister Judah saw it.", insight:"'Turn thou unto me. But she returned not.' The saddest sentence in the chapter is God's unanswered invitation."},
{q:"What had God given backsliding Israel?", options:["A bill of divorce","A new covenant","A double portion","A crown of glory"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:8",verse:"And I saw, when for all the causes whereby backsliding Israel committed adultery I had put her away, and given her a bill of divorce; yet her treacherous sister Judah feared not, but went and played the harlot also.", insight:"God gave her a bill of divorce, the full legal end; yet His mercy would still go further than His law."},
{q:"Who watched Israel's judgment and did not fear?", options:["Her treacherous sister Judah","Her neighbor Edom","Her cousin Moab","Her brother Ammon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:8",verse:"And I saw, when for all the causes whereby backsliding Israel committed adultery I had put her away, and given her a bill of divorce; yet her treacherous sister Judah feared not, but went and played the harlot also.", insight:"Judah saw Israel divorced for adultery and played the harlot also; warnings wasted on others are warnings wasted on us."},
{q:"With what did Judah commit adultery?", options:["With stones and with stocks","With silver and with gold","With horses and with chariots","With songs and with dances"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:9",verse:"And it came to pass through the lightness of her whoredom, that she defiled the land, and committed adultery with stones and with stocks.", insight:"Stones and stocks, dead idols of rock and wood; adultery with things that cannot love back."},
{q:"How did Judah turn to the LORD?", options:["Not with her whole heart, but feignedly","With fasting and with weeping","With burnt offerings and incense","With songs of praise and thanksgiving"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:10",verse:"And yet for all this her treacherous sister Judah hath not turned unto me with her whole heart, but feignedly, saith the LORD.", insight:"Feigned repentance is the most dangerous kind; a half turned heart is still a turned away heart."},
{q:"Which sister is declared more justified?", options:["Backsliding Israel, more than treacherous Judah","Treacherous Judah, more than backsliding Israel","Both are justified equally","Neither is justified at all"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:11",verse:"And the LORD said unto me, The backsliding Israel hath justified herself more than treacherous Judah.", insight:"Open rebellion proved more honest than hypocritical religion; God prefers the honest sinner to the pious pretender."},
{q:"Where must Jeremiah proclaim the call to return?", options:["Toward the north","Toward the south","Toward the sea","Toward the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:12",verse:"Go and proclaim these words toward the north, and say, Return, thou backsliding Israel, saith the LORD; and I will not cause mine anger to fall upon you: for I am merciful, saith the LORD, and I will not keep anger for ever.", insight:"The call goes north, where exiled Israel was scattered; God's mercy travels to find the banished."},
{q:"What one thing does God ask of the backslider?", options:["Only acknowledge thine iniquity","Only offer a thousand rams","Only fast for forty days","Only build an altar of stone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:13",verse:"Only acknowledge thine iniquity, that thou hast transgressed against the LORD thy God, and hast scattered thy ways to the strangers under every green tree, and ye have not obeyed my voice, saith the LORD.", insight:"Only acknowledge; the whole way back begins with one honest word about your sin."},
{q:"What will God give the returning people?", options:["Pastors according to His heart, to feed them with knowledge and understanding","Princes according to their wealth, to rule with an iron hand","Prophets according to their dreams, to speak smooth things","Priests according to their lineage, to offer daily sacrifice"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:15",verse:"And I will give you pastors according to mine heart, which shall feed you with knowledge and understanding.", insight:"God gives shepherds after His own heart; true pastors feed with knowledge, not flattery."},
{q:"What will they say no more in those days?", options:["The ark of the covenant of the LORD","The law of Moses our father","The temple of Solomon the king","The songs of David the shepherd"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:16",verse:"And it shall come to pass, when ye be multiplied and increased in the land, in those days, saith the LORD, they shall say no more, The ark of the covenant of the LORD: neither shall it come to mind: neither shall they remember it; neither shall they visit it; neither shall that be done any more.", insight:"The ark will not even come to mind; God's presence will so fill everything that the symbol is forgotten."},
{q:"What shall Jerusalem be called?", options:["The throne of the LORD","The city of the great king","The joy of the whole earth","The mother of nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:17",verse:"At that time they shall call Jerusalem the throne of the LORD; and all the nations shall be gathered unto it, to the name of the LORD, to Jerusalem: neither shall they walk any more after the imagination of their evil heart.", insight:"Jerusalem the throne of the LORD; the city of judgment becomes the seat of His reign."},
{q:"What reunion is promised?", options:["The house of Judah shall walk with the house of Israel","The house of David shall rule the nations","The house of Levi shall keep the temple","The house of Joseph shall inherit the land"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:18",verse:"In those days the house of Judah shall walk with the house of Israel, and they shall come together out of the land of the north to the land that I have given for an inheritance unto your fathers.", insight:"Judah and Israel, long divided, shall walk together out of the north; God's healing reunites what sin split."},
{q:"What does God promise the returning children?", options:["I will heal your backslidings","I will give you the lands of the nations","I will make you rulers over many","I will hide you from all trouble"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 3:22",verse:"Return, ye backsliding children, and I will heal your backslidings. Behold, we come unto thee; for thou art the LORD our God.", insight:"'I will heal your backslidings'; return is met not with a lecture but with a cure."}
]},
{n:4, name:"Break Up Your Fallow Ground", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 4:3",verse:"For thus saith the LORD to the men of Judah and Jerusalem, Break up your fallow ground, and sow not among thorns."}, facts:["Did you know 'fallow ground' was hardened, untilled soil? God uses farming to say repentance must break up a hard heart before truth can be sown (Jeremiah 4:3).","Did you know Jeremiah's vision of the earth 'without form, and void' deliberately echoes Genesis 1:2, picturing judgment as creation undone (Jeremiah 4:23)?","Did you know that in the middle of the desolation God promises, 'yet will I not make a full end,' threading mercy through judgment (Jeremiah 4:27)?"], questions:[
{q:"What is the condition for Israel's return?", options:["Return unto me, and put away thine abominations out of my sight","Offer a thousand burnt offerings","Build the walls of Jerusalem","Gather the elders in the gate"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:1",verse:"If thou wilt return, O Israel, saith the LORD, return unto me: and if thou wilt put away thine abominations out of my sight, then shalt thou not remove.", insight:"Return has two moves: come back to Me, and put the idols out of sight; repentance both approaches and abandons."},
{q:"How must they swear 'The LORD liveth'?", options:["In truth, in judgment, and in righteousness","In haste, in fear, and in trembling","In secret, in silence, and in sorrow","In song, in dance, and in feasting"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:2",verse:"And thou shalt swear, The LORD liveth, in truth, in judgment, and in righteousness; and the nations shall bless themselves in him, and in him shall they glory.", insight:"Even an oath must be true, just, and righteous; God cares how we say His name, not only that we say it."},
{q:"What must be circumcised?", options:["Themselves to the LORD: the foreskins of their heart","Their sons on the eighth day","The priests before the altar","The firstborn of their flocks"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:4",verse:"Circumcise yourselves to the LORD, and take away the foreskins of your heart, ye men of Judah and inhabitants of Jerusalem: lest my fury come forth like fire, and burn that none can quench it, because of the evil of your doings.", insight:"Circumcision of the heart, not just the flesh; God wants the inside cut clean, not the outside marked."},
{q:"What must be blown in the land?", options:["The trumpet","The silver bells","The ram's horn of jubilee","The pipes of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:5",verse:"Declare ye in Judah, and publish in Jerusalem; and say, Blow ye the trumpet in the land: cry, gather together, and say, Assemble yourselves, and let us go into the defenced cities.", insight:"The trumpet blows for war, not worship; judgment was coming and the watchmen had to sound it."},
{q:"From where will evil come?", options:["From the north, with great destruction","From Egypt, with chariots","From the sea, with ships","From the desert, with a dry wind"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:6",verse:"Set up the standard toward Zion: retire, stay not: for I will bring evil from the north, and a great destruction.", insight:"Evil from the north, a great destruction; God names the storm's direction so no one can claim surprise."},
{q:"What beast pictures the invader?", options:["A lion come up from his thicket","A bear robbed of her whelps","A wolf of the evening","A leopard by the waters"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:7",verse:"The lion is come up from his thicket, and the destroyer of the Gentiles is on his way; he is gone forth from his place to make thy land desolate; and thy cities shall be laid waste, without an inhabitant.", insight:"A lion leaving its thicket is done hiding; Babylon was done hiding too."},
{q:"How must the people mourn?", options:["Gird with sackcloth, lament and howl","Wash with water and anoint with oil","Clothe in crimson and deck with gold","Sit in silence for seven days"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:8",verse:"For this gird you with sackcloth, lament and howl: for the fierce anger of the LORD is not turned back from us.", insight:"Sackcloth, lamenting, howling; real repentance looks like grief, not like a ceremony."},
{q:"What will happen to the king's heart?", options:["It shall perish, and the hearts of the princes with it","It shall be strengthened for battle","It shall turn to the LORD in prayer","It shall be hidden in the palace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:9",verse:"And it shall come to pass at that day, saith the LORD, that the heart of the king shall perish, and the heart of the princes; and the priests shall be astonished, and the prophets shall wonder.", insight:"When judgment falls, even royal hearts perish; no rank steadies a heart that God has judged."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah cry to God?", options:["Surely thou hast greatly deceived this people, saying, Ye shall have peace","Surely thou hast forgotten thy covenant with David","Surely thou hast forsaken the house of Jacob","Surely thou hast hidden thy face from Zion"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:10",verse:"Then said I, Ah, Lord GOD! surely thou hast greatly deceived this people and Jerusalem, saying, Ye shall have peace; whereas the sword reacheth unto the soul.", insight:"Jeremiah wrestles honestly with God over the false prophets' promise of peace; even prophets may bring God their hardest questions."},
{q:"How swift are the invader's horses?", options:["Swifter than eagles","Swifter than the wind","Swifter than chariots of iron","Swifter than arrows in flight"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:13",verse:"Behold, he shall come up as clouds, and his chariots shall be as a whirlwind: his horses are swifter than eagles. Woe unto us! for we are spoiled.", insight:"Horses swifter than eagles, chariots like a whirlwind; no one outruns the judgment God sends."},
{q:"What must Jerusalem wash?", options:["Her heart from wickedness, that she may be saved","Her hands in the laver of brass","Her garments in the fuller's soap","Her streets with the latter rain"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:14",verse:"O Jerusalem, wash thine heart from wickedness, that thou mayest be saved. How long shall thy vain thoughts lodge within thee?", insight:"Wash the heart, not the hands; salvation starts inside, where the vain thoughts lodge."},
{q:"From where is affliction published?", options:["From Dan and from mount Ephraim","From Bethel and from Gilgal","From Hebron and from Beersheba","From Joppa and from Ashdod"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:15",verse:"For a voice declareth from Dan, and publisheth affliction from mount Ephraim.", insight:"Dan is the far north, Ephraim the center; the alarm sounds from the border to the heart of the land."},
{q:"How are the besiegers described?", options:["As keepers of a field, round about her","As reapers in the harvest","As shepherds with their flocks","As merchants in the market"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:17",verse:"As keepers of a field, are they against her round about; because she hath been rebellious against me, saith the LORD.", insight:"Like keepers watching a field, the enemy rings the city; rebellion had made Jerusalem a harvest for others."},
{q:"What procured their ruin?", options:["Their way and their doings","The sins of their fathers only","The counsel of false prophets","The weakness of their king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:18",verse:"Thy way and thy doings have procured these things unto thee; this is thy wickedness, because it is bitter, because it reacheth unto thine heart.", insight:"'Thy way and thy doings have procured these things'; their ruin was homegrown, bitter because it reached the heart."},
{q:"What does Jeremiah cry in his anguish?", options:["My bowels, my bowels! I am pained at my very heart","My hands, my hands! they tremble with fear","My eyes, my eyes! they are dim with tears","My feet, my feet! they stumble in the dark"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:19",verse:"My bowels, my bowels! I am pained at my very heart; my heart maketh a noise in me; I cannot hold my peace, because thou hast heard, O my soul, the sound of the trump, the alarm of war.", insight:"The prophet feels the coming war in his own body; a true messenger weeps over the message he must preach."},
{q:"What is cried?", options:["Destruction upon destruction","Peace upon peace","Blessing upon blessing","Victory upon victory"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:20",verse:"Destruction upon destruction is cried; for the whole land is spoiled: suddenly are my tents spoiled, and my curtains in a moment.", insight:"Destruction upon destruction; judgment falls in waves, and each wave cries out the next."},
{q:"What are they wise and unwise in?", options:["Wise to do evil, but to do good they have no knowledge","Wise in counsel, but weak in battle","Wise as serpents, but gentle as doves","Wise in the law, but slow to keep it"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:22",verse:"For my people is foolish, they have not known me; they are sottish children, and they have none understanding: they are wise to do evil, but to do good they have no knowledge.", insight:"Clever at evil, clueless at good; sin sharpens the mind for wrong and dulls it for right."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah behold?", options:["The earth without form and void, and the heavens with no light","The sea dried up and the rivers turned to blood","The sun darkened and the moon turned to sackcloth","The mountains smoking like a furnace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:23",verse:"I beheld the earth, and, lo, it was without form, and void; and the heavens, and they had no light.", insight:"'Without form, and void' echoes Genesis 1:2; judgment unmakes the world, creation running backward."},
{q:"What mercy limits the desolation?", options:["Yet will I not make a full end","Yet will I spare the temple","Yet will I hide the remnant in Egypt","Yet will I delay for forty years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:27",verse:"For thus hath the LORD said, The whole land shall be desolate; yet will I not make a full end.", insight:"'Yet will I not make a full end'; even in desolation, God draws a line that judgment may not cross."},
{q:"What is in vain for the spoiled city?", options:["To clothe herself with crimson, deck with gold, and paint her face","To gird herself with sackcloth and ashes","To cry unto the LORD in the night","To send ambassadors to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 4:30",verse:"And when thou art spoiled, what wilt thou do? Though thou clothest thyself with crimson, though thou deckest thee with ornaments of gold, though thou rentest thy face with painting, in vain shalt thou make thyself fair; thy lovers will despise thee, they will seek thy life.", insight:"Crimson, gold, and painted eyes cannot charm judgment; her lovers will despise her and seek her life."}
]},
{n:5, name:"If Ye Can Find a Man", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 5:1",verse:"Run ye to and fro through the streets of Jerusalem, and see now, and know, and seek in the broad places thereof, if ye can find a man, if there be any that executeth judgment, that seeketh the truth; and I will pardon it."}, facts:["Did you know God offered to pardon all Jerusalem for the sake of one man who executed judgment and sought the truth, yet none could be found (Jeremiah 5:1)?","Did you know Jeremiah names three predators for Judah's punishment: a lion out of the forest, a wolf of the evenings, and a leopard watching over the cities (Jeremiah 5:6)?","Did you know God set the sand as the sea's bound by a perpetual decree, and asks why the people who see the waves obey will not tremble before Him (Jeremiah 5:22)?"], questions:[
{q:"Where must they run to and fro?", options:["Through the streets of Jerusalem","Through the fields of Bethlehem","Through the gates of Samaria","Through the villages of Galilee"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:1",verse:"Run ye to and fro through the streets of Jerusalem, and see now, and know, and seek in the broad places thereof, if ye can find a man, if there be any that executeth judgment, that seeketh the truth; and I will pardon it.", insight:"God sends them running through Jerusalem's streets; the search for one righteous man begins at home."},
{q:"What one man would win pardon for the city?", options:["One that executeth judgment and seeketh the truth","One that offereth a thousand sacrifices","One that buildeth the walls higher","One that payeth the temple tax"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:1",verse:"Run ye to and fro through the streets of Jerusalem, and see now, and know, and seek in the broad places thereof, if ye can find a man, if there be any that executeth judgment, that seeketh the truth; and I will pardon it.", insight:"One just, truth seeking man could have saved the city; God bargains low, and still finds the shelves empty."},
{q:"How do they swear 'The LORD liveth'?", options:["Falsely","In truth","With trembling","In secret"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:2",verse:"And though they say, The LORD liveth; surely they swear falsely.", insight:"God's name on lying lips; an oath is only as true as the heart that swears it."},
{q:"What are their faces like?", options:["Harder than a rock","Softer than clay","Brighter than the sun","Paler than the moon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:3",verse:"O LORD, are not thine eyes upon the truth? thou hast stricken them, but they have not grieved; thou hast consumed them, but they have refused to receive correction: they have made their faces harder than a rock; they have refused to return.", insight:"Faces harder than a rock; correction had hammered them, and they only hardened."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah find among the great men?", options:["They too had broken the yoke and burst the bonds","They kept the law of the LORD","They wept for the sins of the city","They hid the prophets in caves"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:5",verse:"I will get me unto the great men, and will speak unto them; for they have known the way of the LORD, and the judgment of their God: but these have altogether broken the yoke, and burst the bonds.", insight:"Jeremiah hoped the great men knew better; they had broken the yoke too. Rank is no refuge from rebellion."},
{q:"What three beasts will punish them?", options:["A lion out of the forest, a wolf of the evenings, and a leopard","A bear, a serpent, and a dragon","A young lion, a wild ox, and a vulture","A jackal, a hyena, and an adder"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:6",verse:"Wherefore a lion out of the forest shall slay them, and a wolf of the evenings shall spoil them, a leopard shall watch over their cities: every one that goeth out thence shall be torn in pieces: because their transgressions are many, and their backslidings are increased.", insight:"Lion, wolf, leopard; God musters the wild against a people grown wilder than beasts."},
{q:"What did fullness lead them to?", options:["When fed to the full, they committed adultery","When fed to the full, they gave thanks","When fed to the full, they shared with the poor","When fed to the full, they rested in peace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:7",verse:"How shall I pardon thee for this? thy children have forsaken me, and sworn by them that are no gods: when I had fed them to the full, they then committed adultery, and assembled themselves by troops in the harlots’ houses.", insight:"Full stomachs, empty morals; prosperity unthanked curdles into sin."},
{q:"To what are they compared in their lust?", options:["Fed horses in the morning, neighing after his neighbour's wife","Hungry wolves circling the fold","Wild goats on the high hills","Restless waves of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:8",verse:"They were as fed horses in the morning: every one neighed after his neighbour’s wife.", insight:"Fed horses neighing for another's wife; appetite fed becomes appetite enslaved."},
{q:"What question does God ask?", options:["Shall I not visit for these things?","Shall I not hide my face forever?","Shall I not forget their iniquity?","Shall I not send them into exile?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:9",verse:"Shall I not visit for these things? saith the LORD: and shall not my soul be avenged on such a nation as this?", insight:"'Shall I not visit?' is God's holy must; a just God cannot look at such things and do nothing."},
{q:"What must be taken from her walls?", options:["Her battlements, for they are not the LORD's","Her gates, for they are shut","Her towers, for they are high","Her stones, for they are heavy"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:10",verse:"Go ye up upon her walls, and destroy; but make not a full end: take away her battlements; for they are not the LORD’S.", insight:"Take the battlements, 'for they are not the LORD's'; what God never owned, He will not defend."},
{q:"What did they say of the LORD?", options:["It is not he; neither shall evil come upon us","He is too far to hear us","He has forgotten his covenant","He delights in our offerings"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:12",verse:"They have belied the LORD, and said, It is not he; neither shall evil come upon us; neither shall we see sword nor famine:", insight:"'It is not he'; denying God's hand in judgment is the last lie before the fall."},
{q:"What shall the false prophets become?", options:["Wind","Dust","Smoke","A fading flower"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:13",verse:"And the prophets shall become wind, and the word is not in them: thus shall it be done unto them.", insight:"Prophets with no word become wind; empty preaching blows away with the first storm."},
{q:"What will God's words become in Jeremiah's mouth?", options:["Fire, and the people wood, to be devoured","Honey, and the people bread, to be fed","A sword, and the people chaff, to be scattered","A hammer, and the people stone, to be broken"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:14",verse:"Wherefore thus saith the LORD God of hosts, Because ye speak this word, behold, I will make my words in thy mouth fire, and this people wood, and it shall devour them.", insight:"God's word as fire, the people as wood; truth does not tickle, it consumes."},
{q:"What nation will God bring upon them?", options:["A mighty, ancient nation whose language they know not","A small, weak nation from the desert","A friendly nation bearing gifts","A neighboring nation seeking peace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:15",verse:"Lo, I will bring a nation upon you from far, O house of Israel, saith the LORD: it is a mighty nation, it is an ancient nation, a nation whose language thou knowest not, neither understandest what they say.", insight:"A far nation with an unknown tongue; judgment often arrives speaking a language we never learned."},
{q:"What is their quiver like?", options:["An open sepulchre","A full barn","A deep well","A shut door"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:16",verse:"Their quiver is as an open sepulchre, they are all mighty men.", insight:"A quiver like an open grave; every arrow they loose carries death, starting with their own."},
{q:"What will the invaders eat up?", options:["Harvest, bread, flocks, herds, vines, and fig trees","Gold, silver, and precious stones","The books of the law","The vessels of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:17",verse:"And they shall eat up thine harvest, and thy bread, which thy sons and thy daughters should eat: they shall eat up thy flocks and thine herds: they shall eat up thy vines and thy fig trees: they shall impoverish thy fenced cities, wherein thou trustedst, with the sword.", insight:"They will eat what sons and daughters should have eaten; sin devours the children's portion first."},
{q:"What mercy is repeated?", options:["I will not make a full end with you","I will spare the city walls","I will hide you in the wilderness","I will send you back to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:18",verse:"Nevertheless in those days, saith the LORD, I will not make a full end with you.", insight:"Again the limit: 'I will not make a full end'; God's judgments discipline, they do not annihilate His people."},
{q:"How are the people described?", options:["They have eyes and see not; ears and hear not","They have hands and work not; feet and walk not","They have mouths and speak not; hearts and feel not","They have strength and use it not; wisdom and hide it"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:21",verse:"Hear now this, O foolish people, and without understanding; which have eyes, and see not; which have ears, and hear not:", insight:"Eyes that see not, ears that hear not; the most pitiable blindness is the kind that refuses to look."},
{q:"What bound did God place for the sea?", options:["The sand, by a perpetual decree","The mountains, by an oath","The clouds, by a covenant","The stars, by a statute"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:22",verse:"Fear ye not me? saith the LORD: will ye not tremble at my presence, which have placed the sand for the bound of the sea by a perpetual decree, that it cannot pass it: and though the waves thereof toss themselves, yet can they not prevail; though they roar, yet can they not pass over it?", insight:"Sand holds back the sea at God's decree; if the ocean obeys its bound, why will not His people tremble?"},
{q:"What is the state of prophets and priests?", options:["The prophets prophesy falsely, and the priests bear rule by their means","The prophets are silent, and the priests have fled","The prophets weep, and the priests repent","The prophets dream, and the priests sleep"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 5:31",verse:"The prophets prophesy falsely, and the priests bear rule by their means; and my people love to have it so: and what will ye do in the end thereof?", insight:"False prophets, power hungry priests, and a people who love it so; corruption needs an audience to thrive."}
]}
]);
